<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="agent-options" title="Agent options">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        The agent's options are the text after the first <code>=</code> on
        <code>-javaagent</code>. They are read last, so an ordinary key on the command line beats
        the same system property.
        <code>dump</code> and the policy keys are exceptions; <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration
        keys</a> has the precedence rule in full.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="bash">
OPTS=explain=true,onError=report

java -javaagent:aether-weaver-agent-%version%.jar=$OPTS \
     -cp app.jar app.Main
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="How the string is read" id="grammar">
        <p>
            Comma-separated <code>key=value</code> pairs, with <code>aether.weaver.</code> implied
            on every key.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Written</td><td>How it parses</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>onError=report</code></td><td>One pair. The prefix is added before the lookup</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dump=/tmp/a=b</code></td><td>The first <code>=</code> splits it; later ones stay in the value</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>explain = true</code></td><td>Both halves are trimmed, and an empty pair is skipped</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dump=/a,dump=/b</code></td><td>The last value given for a key is the one used</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>verifcation=report</code></td><td>Unknown; the nearest scalar key is offered when close enough</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>report</code></td><td>No <code>=</code> at all. Give the key as well</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            The last two are <code>AW2310</code>, and nothing is coerced. A value the parser
            cannot read leaves that key unset, so a system property or the default decides it
            instead. <code>enabled=ture</code> does not read as <code>false</code>. A
            <code>dump</code> value is the exception. An unusable path throws instead, and no
            later fragment in that source is read.
            <a href="diagnostics-environment.topic">AW2xxx</a> has the five ways it is raised.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                Nothing escapes the comma, so a multi-valued key cannot be written here.
                <code>tags.include=audit,metrics</code> parses as <code>tags.include=audit</code>
                followed by a fragment <code>metrics</code>. Set that key as a system property
                instead, where the whole list survives:
            </p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
java -Daether.weaver.tags.include=audit,metrics \
     -javaagent:aether-weaver-agent-%version%.jar \
     -jar app.jar
            </code-block>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The keys it takes" id="keys">
        <p>
            Every key the parser accepts, spelled without the prefix.
            <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has the accepted values, the
            defaults and the layer order.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Key</td><td>What it changes here</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>enabled</code></td><td>Off installs no transformer and says so in one line</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>explain</code></td><td>Prints the plan at install time, not at the end</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>onError</code></td><td>Whether a class that fails to weave halts the JVM</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>verification</code></td><td>What the engine does with bytes its verifier refuses</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dump</code></td><td>Writes the before and after bytes of each woven class</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>phase</code></td><td>Nothing; it reaches the closing line and no further</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tags.include</code></td><td>Which discovered weaves reach the plan</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tags.exclude</code></td><td>Which discovered weaves are dropped from it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>policy.allowSigned</code></td><td>Nothing here; the class-loader driver reads it instead</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>policy.allowPackage</code></td><td>Nothing enforces it; only the summary line notes it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>weave[name].enabled</code></td><td>Keeps or drops one weave, whatever its tags say</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>weave[name].priority</code></td><td>Nothing; stored but unread</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>injector[name].enabled</code></td><td>Nothing; discovery reads <code>weave[name].enabled</code> instead</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            A name inside brackets is taken verbatim and matched against nothing, so a misspelt
            weave never applies and says nothing about it. Check it against the manifest that
            declared it; <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> covers the
            search.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The two entry points" id="entry-points">
        <p>
            One class answers both. A flag inside it is false from <code>premain</code> and true
            from <code>agentmain</code>, and that flag gates two things a
            <code>-javaagent</code> run never does.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>What differs</td><td>Under <code>-javaagent</code>, and on a dynamic attach</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Manifest entry</td><td><code>Premain-Class</code>; <code>Agent-Class</code> for an attach</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Already-loaded targets</td><td>Untouched; an attach retransforms what it can</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A structural weave</td><td>Silent unless a target is loaded; then <code>AW2101</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>What <code>explain=true</code> shows</td><td><code>not woven yet</code>; an attach, what each point matched</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The closing line ends</td><td><code>(premain)</code>; <code>(agentmain)</code> for an attach</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Under <code>-javaagent</code> every target is woven as it is defined. Neither the
            retransformation nor <code>AW2101</code> arises there; weave at build time when a
            target is already loaded. An attach raises <code>AW2101</code> for a structural weave
            whose target is already loaded. It raises the same code again if the JVM then
            refuses the retransformation. Attaching to a live JVM is
            <a href="runtime-weaving.topic">Weaving in a running application</a>, and the flag on the command
            line is <a href="load-time-weaving.topic">Weaving at load time</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
            The closing line is your confirmation. It names the version, the weave and target
            counts, the plan fingerprint and the settings as resolved.
            <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> says what a fingerprint covers.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What reaches your console" id="output">
        <p>
            Diagnostics go into one list, and the agent empties it at whichever of four points
            ends the installation:
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>the configuration switched weaving off;</p></li>
            <li><p>discovery came back with no weave to apply;</p></li>
            <li><p>the plan could not be built, which rethrows and, under
                <code>-javaagent</code>, aborts the JVM before <code>main</code>;</p></li>
            <li><p>the closing line, once the transformer is installed.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            Nothing empties the list again. Under <code>-javaagent</code>, everything raised from
            here on is unprinted. That includes <code>AW4090</code> for a
            failed weave and <code>AW2202</code> for a class already woven by another plan.
            Retransforming an already-loaded target under <code>agentmain</code> prints its
            diagnostics before the closing line. Only a class defined after that line stays
            unprinted. Weave at build time when you need those
            messages; <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has the run that prints them, and
            <a href="diagnostics-verification.topic">AW4xxx</a> the codes.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="onError=fail, the default">
                <p>
                    Three lines naming the class and the policy go to standard error, then the
                    failure's stack trace. The JVM is then halted with status 70 — halted rather
                    than exited, because this runs on a class-loading thread. No shutdown hook
                    runs, and nothing buffered elsewhere is flushed.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="onError=report">
                <p>
                    The JVM keeps the bytes it already had, that one class loads unwoven, and the
                    application carries on. Give the message and a <code>dump</code> directory to
                    the <a href="%issues%">issue tracker</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

